<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>
    {{- page_title -}}
    {%- unless page_title contains shop.name %} | {{ shop.name }}{% endunless -%}
  </title>
  {%- if page_description -%}
    <meta name="description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">
  {%- endif -%}
  <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">
  {%- comment -%} Mulish, self-hosted. Replaces three render-blocking requests to a
      third party (two preconnects plus the stylesheet, which itself then fetched the
      font). One variable woff2, latin subset, covering every weight 400-900 — Google
      serves the same file for each static weight anyway, so this costs nothing extra
      and cannot go stale. Preloaded because @font-face inside a stylesheet is only
      discovered after that stylesheet parses. {%- endcomment -%}
  {{ 'mulish-variable.woff2' | asset_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', crossorigin: 'anonymous' }}
  <style>
    @font-face {
      font-family: 'Mulish';
      font-style: normal;
      font-weight: 400 900;
      font-display: swap;
      src: url({{ 'mulish-variable.woff2' | asset_url }}) format('woff2');
    }
  </style>
  {{ 'base.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  {%- comment -%} The four colour settings had no effect on anything: base.css declared the
      tokens with literal hexes and every inline style hardcoded its own copy. This block is
      what connects the theme editor to the page. It must come after base.css so it wins.

      The --c-on-* tokens are the text colour that sits ON each green, and they are computed,
      not fixed. White on the brand lime measures 2.04:1 against a 4.5:1 AA requirement, so
      the theme was failing contrast on ADD TO BAG and every other primary button. Deriving
      the foreground from perceived brightness fixes that and keeps it fixed: a merchant who
      picks a dark green in the editor gets white text back automatically, and one who picks
      a pale one keeps dark text. Do not replace these with a literal. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign c_primary = settings.color_primary | default: '#82C914'
    assign c_primary_hover = settings.color_primary_hover | default: '#6BA30F'
    assign c_dark = settings.color_dark | default: '#3F6B4F'
    assign c_accent = settings.color_accent | default: '#92C83F'

    assign dark_ink = '#2A2B2A'
    assign light_ink = '#FFFFFF'

    assign on_primary = light_ink
    assign b = c_primary | color_brightness
    if b > 145
      assign on_primary = dark_ink
    endif

    # Merchant override. The design handoff specified white on the lime, which measures
    # 2.04:1 — under half of AA. Kept as an explicit, reversible choice rather than
    # hard-coded, so switching the setting to 'auto' restores the accessible pairing
    # with no code change. setup/verify/contrast.py reports what this costs.
    if settings.button_text_white == 'white'
      assign on_primary = light_ink
    endif

    assign on_accent = light_ink
    assign b = c_accent | color_brightness
    if b > 145
      assign on_accent = dark_ink
    endif

    assign on_dark = light_ink
    assign b = c_dark | color_brightness
    if b > 145
      assign on_dark = dark_ink
    endif

    # The brand lime is a background colour, not an ink. On white it measures 2.04:1,
    # so prices, links and category names were all failing AA. Darken the SAME hue
    # until it passes rather than substituting a different green, which keeps the
    # brand reading intact. Steps of 6% reach AA for the shipped lime at #53820D
    # (4.60:1) and self-correct if the merchant picks something else.
    assign c_primary_text = c_primary
    for i in (1..10)
      assign ct = c_primary_text | color_contrast: '#FFFFFF'
      if ct >= 4.5
        break
      endif
      assign c_primary_text = c_primary_text | color_darken: 6
    endfor
  -%}
  <style>
    :root {
      --c-primary: {{ c_primary }};
      --c-primary-hover: {{ c_primary_hover }};
      --c-dark: {{ c_dark }};
      --c-accent: {{ c_accent }};
      --c-on-primary: {{ on_primary }};
      --c-on-accent: {{ on_accent }};
      --c-on-dark: {{ on_dark }};
      --c-primary-text: {{ c_primary_text }};
    }
  </style>
  {%- comment -%} theme.js is a static asset and can't read Liquid — hand it the cart routes so they survive a locale/market path prefix {%- endcomment -%}
  <script>window.mccRoutes = { cart_url: {{ routes.cart_url | json }}, cart_add_url: {{ routes.cart_add_url | json }}, cart_change_url: {{ routes.cart_change_url | json }}, predictive_search_url: {{ routes.predictive_search_url | json }}, product_recommendations_url: {{ routes.product_recommendations_url | json }} };</script>
  <script src="{{ 'theme.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
  {{ content_for_header }}
</head>
<body>
  {% sections 'header-group' %}
  <main id="main" style="width:100%; overflow-x:hidden; background:#ffffff;">
    {{ content_for_layout }}
  </main>
  {% sections 'footer-group' %}
  {%- comment -%} JSON-LD for the page type. Emitted in the body rather than the
      head so it cannot interfere with content_for_header. {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'structured-data' %}
  {%- comment -%} Last in the DOM so it is one Tab from wherever the visitor is,
      and cannot push page content around on load. {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'consent-banner' %}
</body>
</html>
